Now I understand the flow. The input is an ASS subtitle file. When it contains a `[Fonts]` section, the font data lines are accumulated in `fontdata`, and when a new `fontname:` line appears or at the end of processing, `decode_font` is called.

Let me look at the `decode_chars` function more carefully to understand the overflow:

```c
static unsigned char *decode_chars(const unsigned char *src,
                                   unsigned char *dst, size_t cnt_in)
{
    uint32_t value = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < cnt_in; i++)
        value |= (uint32_t) ((src[i] - 33u) & 63) << 6 * (3 - i);

    *dst++ = value >> 16;
    if (cnt_in >= 3)
        *dst++ = value >> 8 & 0xff;
    if (cnt_in >= 4)
        *dst++ = value & 0xff;
    return dst;
}
```

Wait, I need to re-examine the buffer allocation:

```c
buf = malloc(size / 4 * 3 + FFMAX(size % 4 - 1, 0));
```

For `size % 4 == 2`: allocation = `size/4 * 3 + 1`. The loop runs `size/4` times with `cnt_in=4`, writing 3 bytes each = `size/4 * 3`. Then `decode_chars(p, q, 2)` writes 1 byte. Total = `size/4 * 3 + 1`. OK.

For `size % 4 == 3`: allocation = `size/4 * 3 + 2`. The loop runs `size/4` times with `cnt_in=4`, writing 3 bytes each = `size/4 * 3`. Then `decode_chars(p, q, 3)` writes 2 bytes. Total = `size/4 * 3 + 2`. OK.

